        "bodyTr": "Shadows of uncertainty have fallen across the global artificial intelligence ecosystem following a series of infidelity scandals involving Jensen Huang, CEO of Nvidia. In the aftermath, concerns have grown that the fallout could affect our economy, which is more dependent on semiconductors than ever before. According to local U.S. media reports, Nvidia's board of directors discussed the dismissal of CEO Huang on the 11th (local time). As Huang's leadership, which has solidified a de facto one-man rule, wobbles, Nvidia's stock price has been plummeting daily. This is not a distant fire for us to watch from across the river. Nvidia is far more than a simple GPU supplier; based on distinctive technological prowess and formidable financial resources, the company has essentially led the creation of ecosystem demand. Domestically, it is a key buyer of high bandwidth memory (HBM) produced by Samsung Electronics and SK Hynix, and has been pushing collaborative projects with major corporations including Hyundai Motor, LG, and Doosan in the physical AI sector. If management is suddenly replaced, variables could emerge in subsequent investments and joint development schedules.\n\nWhat stands out is that this scandal has evolved beyond a mere infidelity issue into allegations of involvement with Chinese spies, and the United States is moving to further tighten control over semiconductor supply chains. In particular, Congress is simultaneously deliberating the \"AI Surveillance Act,\" which strengthens congressional control when exporting AI semiconductors; the \"Match Act,\" which prevents allied nations from exporting semiconductor manufacturing equipment to China; and the \"Chip Security Act,\" which tracks the location of chips sold overseas to prevent smuggling. These are variables that carry significant implications for domestic companies, given their substantial export share to China and memory production operations within China itself. The problem is that such uncertainty does not remain merely an industry-level issue. Semiconductors account for nearly half of South Korea's total exports. Depending on the direction of related industries, exports, growth rates, and even tax revenues could be shaken. Against a backdrop where concerns about AI overinvestment are already surfacing and worries loom that a Democratic landslide in U.S. midterm elections could put the brakes on local data center construction, financial market anxieties are mounting. It is time for both corporations and the nation to reexamine risk factors and devise measures to alleviate excessive concentration.",
